---
// Policy: removal and takedown (the page /removal/; docs/CONTRIBUTIONS.md, "Request a removal" and
// "Moderation"; oscr/moderation.py, the rules that decide; oscr/jobs.py `oscr reports`;
// oscr/migrations/0008_withheld.sql).
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { REVIEW_DAYS } from "../../lib/moderation";

const toc = [
  { id: "scope", text: "What may be removed" },
  { id: "who", text: "Who may ask" },
  { id: "how", text: "How to ask" },
  { id: "decisions", text: "How requests are decided" },
  { id: "effect", text: "What an accepted request does" },
  { id: "permanent", text: "What cannot be undone elsewhere" },
  { id: "rejected", text: "A rejected request" },
  { id: "back", text: "Coming back" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Removal and takedown"
  description={`${SITE_NAME}'s removal policy: what can be removed, who may ask, how published rules decide each request, and what a removal does.`}
  group="policies"
  path="/policies/removal/"
  parents={[{ text: "Policies", href: "/policies/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    {SITE_NAME} publishes records of papers and copies of code that their licences allow. Anyone may still ask for a
    record, or part of it, to be removed: published rules decide every request, in the safe direction, and the answer
    comes back in words. There is no human moderator on duty at the moment: what the rules cannot decide waits for the
    operator, {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scope">What may be removed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>a paper's whole record;</li>
    <li>the copies of its authors' scripts, all of them, one repository's, or one file's — or, for code {SITE_NAME} does
      not copy, their display from their source (the files stay listed with their links to the source);</li>
    <li>its tracing map, with its matches, validations and DOI on the site.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A record's links are better corrected than removed, by its authors (<a href="/help/correct/">how</a>). Data about
    you, including the contact details kept privately, follow the <a href="/privacy/#rights">privacy page</a>, through
    the same form.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who may ask</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone signed in: an author of the paper (marked verified when their ORCID iD is on the paper), the holder of the
    rights, a person named in the record, or someone else. Signing in is required until the site has its own bot
    check; it asks for no email address, it lets the answer reach you, and it tells the rules who asks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">How to ask</h2>
  <p>
    From the paper's page, "Request removal", or <a href="/removal/">the removal page</a>: who you are, what to remove,
    why (copyright or licence, personal data, wrongly attributed or not your work, a retracted paper, an incorrect
    record, another reason), a justification of 30 to 2,000 characters, an optional evidence link, and two
    confirmations; then a review before sending (<a href="/help/removal/">the guide</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="decisions">How requests are decided</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE_NAME}'s machine applies published rules to each request within minutes; the{" "}
    <PageLink href="/policies/moderation/">moderation policy</PageLink> gives them in full. In short:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>from a verified author of the paper: applied at once, whatever it names;</li>
    <li>for the copies of code its requester maintains as the repository's owner or a public member of its organization
      on GitHub, or as a maintainer the operator made (not as a contributor): applied at once;</li>
    <li>for copies of the authors' code, for copyright or personal data, from anyone: hidden at once, within daily
      limits, pending the operator's review;</li>
    <li>anything else — a whole record, a tracing map, another reason: nothing is removed; the request waits for the
      operator, {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most, to be read with the record it names, the requester's role and the evidence
      given. Unreviewed, it is closed without removal, with how to ask again.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every decision comes with a few words the requester reads on the request's page and on their account page. The rule
    that made it is recorded, so that the operator can check it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="effect">What an accepted request does</h2>
  <p>
    What it names leaves every public output at the <strong>next nightly publication</strong> (04:17, the operator's local
    time): the site, the DOI lookup, the search, the facts the sign-in uses, and the open data.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A whole record: the paper leaves as an off-topic paper does, everywhere; the operator's computer keeps it, marked
      withdrawn, with when and why, so that the harvester does not bring it back.</li>
    <li>Copies: their text leaves the site's files, the public database and the scripts' dataset (its manifests), and the
      files {SITE_NAME} does not copy are no longer fetched from their source; the files stay listed, with their links,
      and the reader says "withheld at a removal request".</li>
    <li>A map: its section says it is withheld, with neither digest (nothing left to validate), matches, validations nor
      DOI, on the site, in the open data and in the search.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="permanent">What cannot be undone elsewhere</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A copy already in a published Parquet block on Hugging Face stays in that block, since a published block never
      changes: the manifest that points to it is withdrawn, so no reader of the dataset finds it.</li>
    <li>A DOI given to a validated map stays on Zenodo, since a DOI is permanent; the site no longer shows it.</li>
    <li>Copies others made while it was public are beyond the registry's reach.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rejected">A rejected request</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing changes; the requester reads the reason. One request per account and record: a refused or closed request can
    be asked again through the form only in a way the rules decide at once — as a verified author of the paper, as a
    maintainer of the code it names, or for the copies only, for copyright or personal data. The operator may also
    override the rules and accept it. A new fact can be brought to the operator through a correction, or, when you are
    an author, a claim.
  </p>

  <h2 id="back">Coming back</h2>
  <p>
    A record withdrawn or a copy withheld comes back only by the operator's hand: when the operator reverses what the
    rules did (copies hidden without reason, for instance), or at the request of the person who asked for its removal.
  </p>
</Info>
